**One more added after reading Lublin et al.** (Aug 2026), unscheduled — the
week grid is being redone, so this is listed by what it is rather than when it
runs:

| Lab | Source | State |
|---|---|---|
| `sweet-spot/` | Lublin, Handley, Brunell & Grofman, "Minority Success in Non-Majority Minority Districts," *JREP* 5 (2020): 275–298 | **complete** — brief knits to HTML and PDF |

It rebuilds the paper's Figure 1 simulation exactly, then sweeps the standard
deviation the authors call "arbitrarily .03" and shows the sweet spot moving
10.5 points; reprints Table 3A's U.S. House row **with the case counts** (the
40–50% claim rests on n=2 and n=1) and rebuilds it on the 118th Congress, where
the same band holds 20 districts and the forecast holds up; and then asks how
many majority-Black congressional districts there are, getting **six answers
between 8 and 14** from one file. Six districts sit on both sides of
*Bartlett*'s line depending only on the denominator. The paper's actual
mechanism — success peaking at intermediate Republican share — **does not appear
in the 118th**: the quadratic term has p = 0.60, and inside the critical band
the winners' median Trump share is 23.5 against 54.5 for the losers.

The dependent variable is the interesting part. **No federal file records the
race of a member of Congress.** `fetch-historian.py` scrapes the House
Historian's photo gallery and recovers Bioguide IDs from image filenames,
because that is the only machine-reachable form the attribute has. Pairs with
`vote-dilution` (Gingles I), `officeholder-age` (the attribute the Bioguide
*does* record), and `census-api` (files that exist because the VRA needs them).
Borrows presidential-by-district from `house-competition` rather than re-fetching.

**One more added Aug 2026, built but deliberately unplaced:**

| Lab | Week | Source | State |
|---|---|---|---|
| `bellwether/` | **unplaced** | Grofman & Chen 2023, *PRQ* 76(1) | **complete** — brief knits to HTML + PDF |

`bellwether/` tests the bellwether-county myth on county presidential returns
1960–2024. It reproduces Grofman and Chen's two published figures (2% of
counties perfect over 1960–1980, 0.3% over 2000–2020) and then reproduces the
*Wall Street Journal*'s November 2020 list of nineteen bellwethers **exactly**
— searching all 3,081 counties for a perfect 1980–2016 record returns those
nineteen and no others, from a source the *Journal* did not use. Eighteen of
them died in 2020 and the last, Clallam County WA, died in 2024. **Zero perfect
records over the full seventeen elections, under either definition of "the
national winner."**

It has no session yet. Natural homes, in order: **Tue Oct 20** beside
`midterm-loss` (the two argue with each other — one rule is certain about the
sign and useless about the size, the other looks predictive and is not a rule
at all); Session 9 beside `models-markets`; or Session 4 as a second
self-study brief.

> **This is the only lab whose `build-data.R` cannot run unattended**, and the
> reason is the lab's subject. The long series is Algara and Amlani's
> replication archive (doi:10.7910/DVN/DGUMFI), which is free and openly
> licensed but sits behind an AWS WAF: a scripted request returns **HTTP 202
> with an empty body** and `x-amzn-waf-action: challenge`. Not 403 — a
> success-shaped status code and zero bytes, which a careless script would
> record as a successful download. The file must be fetched once in a browser
> into `data/raw/`; the script refuses to run rather than pretend. Every other
> route to a long county series is worse: ICPSR 8611 needs an institutional
> login, CQ Press and Leip are paid. There is no free official national county
> file because **no federal agency counts votes** — the `returns-source`
> argument, arriving as an obstacle rather than a claim.
>
> The hand-carried file is audited two ways, both automatic: against MEDSL's
> independent 2000–2016 compilation (15,562 county-elections, 12 winner
> disagreements at 0.077%, none touching a bellwether) and against the 26
> states' certified 2024 returns already assembled in `county-returns/` (1,909
> counties, every vote total identical). The 12 disagreements are all New York
> fusion voting, Kansas City reporting separately from Jackson County, and one
> transposition — the same fusion problem that bit `parse-clerk.py`.

The NC lab downloads ~110 MB of precinct returns for four elections and
aggregates to county. It reproduces the book's five printed counties exactly,
and finds two errors in the book's statewide row — Table 5.3 gives the 2016
Republican total as 2,289,880 where Table 5.2 and the state both say 2,298,880,
and the 2020 figure is off by one. `build-data.R` re-checks this on every run.

